Date: Mon, 25 Jan 93 19:33:46 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: PPL: Intellectual Property Rights

Mark Venture posts:

>	My recommendation is this.  There may be enough demand from
>inventors for companies to be established which record inventions
>and restrict the access to and usage of those inventions. . . .

This system would face problems if applied to products that are resold
after having been produced under the terms of the private patent agreement.
 Suppose, for example, that Ann pays to view the private patent for a new
type of keyboard.  Thereafter she produces and sells it.  What is to
prevent one of her customers from reverse engineering the keyboard and
producing it independently?  

Perhaps you would have Ann agree not to sell the keyboard without having
attached a contract forbidding reverse engineering.  But large retail
operations will have too many goods on the market to make this contract
practically enforceable.  

I note, however, that Mark's proposal would work a lot like a system of
trade secrets if it were restricted to items that are not resold or
otherwise subject to high risk of reverse engineering.  Franchisers
currently distribute trade secrets, for example, with provisions forbidding
disclosure to third parties, and this seems to work pretty well.

With regard more generally to intellectual property in a PPL, I think we
should not assume that patents and/or copyrights will not be enforced. 
There are currently plenty of treaties among States to enforce
mutually-respected intellectual property rights, and States in an
international sphere stand with regard to one another much like individual
legal systems would in a PPL.  There are fairly good arguments to support
the view that protecting intellectual property rights increases aggregate
welfare.  If so, people may agree to live in legal systems that protect
these rights and that enter into treaties with other legal systems to
cooridinate enforcement across jurisdictional boundaries.

I conclude that we should view intellectual property laws like many other
laws that statists favor:  we should not dismiss them just because they are
currently enforced inefficently by an organization with highly questionable
moral foundations.  Regardless of the fact that statists outlaw murder and
deny victims' restitution, we should not want to legalize murder in a PPL. 
The same is true of less morally loaded laws, like traffic regulations. 
Perhaps the same is also true of intellectual property.


 

