Date: Mon, 25 Jan 93 17:54:22 PST
From: raphael@fx.com (Glen Raphael)
Subject: Re:  PPL: Intellectual Property Rights

sulko-m@acsu.buffalo.edu writes:

>[Under a PPL, inventors] need a way to internalize the benefits enough
>to make it worth their while to put the effort into inventing.  I believe
>it might be possible.
>
>        My recommendation is this.  There may be enough demand from
>inventors for companies to be established which record inventions
>and restrict the access to and usage of those inventions.  These
>inventions may be examined (possibly for a fee), but the examiners
>must agree in advance that they may not benefit from the inventions
>they see without paying the inventor the agreed upon fee, or to disclose
>the ideas without incurring heavy monetary penalties and/or loosing
>the ability to see any record of inventions again.  It could be that
>ostracism from invention-record businesses for misbehavior would be
>enough to keep free-riding companies in line.

I like it. In fact, something very much like this already exists. It's
called the MIT Media Lab. The Media Lab does really cool cutting edge
research projects, and companies like Sony, IBM, and GM pay a sort of
subscription fee for the right to drop in from time to time and see
what's being developed. They pay a larger sum for the right to aim
some research in a particular direction, without really knowing whether
it will be successful or not, and they also pay a larger sum for full
access to a particular researcher or project, or for the right to
exclude others to "their" research. We're talking about basic
research, invention and product development being done in a sort of
floating independent contractor relationship with "access" to it a
closely held, marketable good. If you want to keep your access, you
do business with them on their terms.

Read _The Media Lab: Inventing the Future at MIT_ by Stewart Brandt
for more info. I think you're on the right track; something like the
Media Lab could survive essentially intact in an environment without
government-enforced patents, and might in fact flourish.

