Date: Mon, 25 Jan 93 19:09:10 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PPL: Many Types of Intellectual Property Are Not Protected

The discussions about intellectual property, patents, and copyright in
a PPL setting are interesting.

It's useful to remember--or to realize for the first time--that there
are large areas where there is effectively no recognized intellectual
property law. 

Some examples:

* Basic intellectual ideas. Nobody gets sued for being influenced by,
say, Ayn Rand, and then using her types of reasoning and ideas.
Precise wordings are recognized as being protectable, under the
current system, but not the ideas and concepts.

* Architecture. If I see a house design I really like, I am free to
emulate it, be influenced by it, etc. Frank Lloyd Wright, for example,
could not sue those who "used" his ideas.

* Musical or artistic styles. Andy Warhol could not sue his many
imitators, nor can Bob Dylan sue Mark Knopfler of "Dire Straits."
"Borrowing" of the best ideas/memes is what it's all about.

* Magazines cannot patent or copyright their "ideas." Thus, "Mondo
2000" cannot sue the publishers of "Wired" for essentially copying
them. (If either Steven Levy or Kevin Kelly is now reading this, I'm
just using it as an example!)

* Actors cannot stop others from learning methods and tricks from
their performances and incorporaring them into their own acts.

* Finally, fiction writers. They cannot stop others from being
influenced by their writings. (The cases where it is ruled that a
writer has a "right" to his "universe" and his "characters" is a
disturbing trend. A recent court battle over someone's "sequel" to
"Gone With the Wind," written about a century ago, typifies the
absurdity of some aspects of this.)

Note that deliberate misrepresentation is sometimes a consideration.
The orginal greeting card case that established the modern "look and
feel" approach, in the U.S., had to do specifically with a rival
company that chose scenes and fonts for its greeting cards that made
the cards look, at a distance, very similar to the cards of its
competitor (Hallmark?). In my opinion, this precedent was
well-intentioned, in that it dealt with a form of fraud, but has
devolved into an innnovation-stifling quagmire.

(And fraud is becoming easier to detect, what with reputations,
independent testing agencies, and even digital signatures. It is not
likely that someone will be misled into buying a house thinking it was
a Wright-designed house, to pick one example.)

In all of these cases listed above, substantial investment of time was
needed to come up with ideas, to design buildings, and so on. The
arguments for patents, that inventors need some State-granted monopoly
to recoup their investment and to incentiveize them, apply as well to
these other areas. (I'll accept that there's _some_ difference between
patents on gadgets and patents on artistic performances. Perhaps in a
PPL society there will develop reasonable solutions which comprehend
these differences. My main point in this post is to debunk the notion
that intellectual property laws are essential to the development of
new things.)

Patents and copyrights are not the only way to recoup investments.
Thinkers, actors, artists, and other developers of new things do not
seem to need a coercive state to make a living. 

Reputations of original developers are usually enough to ensure a
significant income stream. As others pointed out a while back, a
State-granted patent system means inventors are most often employed by
corporations, and don't receive "just" rewards. (Not to start a flame
war here...inventors in corporations also don't bear the developments
costs, the risks, etc.)

To make this point clearer, imagine a world in which basic ideas are
patentable. (Some may argue we're already well along this road, with
Apple suing Microsoft for the sin of using a window-based
screen---never mind that the MIT Lisp Machine and Xerox Altos both had
them in the late 1970s--and Lotus suing Borland and others over
spreadsheet ideas.)

In this world, corporations would likely buy up the rights to ideas
and then use the coercive powers of the state to enforce their
property rights. Countersuits would be filed, cross-licensing deals
would be made, and so on. 

Not a pretty picture. 

I hope PPL systems do not evolve in this direction.

P.S. There are noncoercive arrangements involving ostracism (refusal
to transact with) and reputations which are mostly OK. For example, we
on this list have no formal concept of intellectual property, though
innaccurate quoting or other misrepresentation is presumably a
"warnable" offense. Still, if it became clear to the readers of this
list that I had been so thoroughly impressed with, say, Perry
Metzger's style of posting that I was consciously or unconsciously
emulating his style in my own posts, then I can imagine folks would
start ignoring my posts as being too derivative or as (somehow) being
a disagreeable situation. That's as it should be, in my opinion.

An interesting thread!

-Tim May

